# goal Decide what an unsupportable OpenAI parameter costs

This module exists because "not supported" is not one behaviour. The reasoning, and
the split between style and contract, is in
[_.hint#param_policy](../_.hint) — read that first; this file only records the
contract and how to extend it.

# invariant This module imports nothing {#no_imports}

`openai.js` imports it, so importing back would create a cycle for the sake of one
error class. `ParamReporter.report` therefore throws a plain `Error` with `.status`
and `.code` attached, which the HTTP layer already handles the same way.

If this file ever needs a helper, move the helper down here rather than importing up.

# invariant Every parameter is in exactly one bucket {#one_bucket}

`NATIVE`, `EMULATED`, `IGNORED`, `REFUSED`. A name in two buckets, or a REFUSED name
that some other module also handles, is a bug — the buckets are the whole policy and
they must be readable as one list.

Unknown names fall through to ignored **by design**, so the sets do not have to be
exhaustive to be correct.

# rule Adding support for a parameter is a bucket change, not an if {#adding_params}

When something becomes implementable, move its name from `REFUSED`/`IGNORED` into
`EMULATED` or `NATIVE` and implement it — do not special-case it upstream and leave
the name behind. A parameter honoured in `server.js` but still listed as ignored
reports a lie to the caller in `x_acp2api.ignored`.

Promoting a name **out** of `REFUSED` requires that the implementation preserve the
meaning the caller expects. `tools` may only leave that set when real `tool_calls`
come back, not when tools are merely passed to the agent somehow.

# func classify {#classify}

## arg body
The raw request body. Only its **keys** matter, except for `n`, where `1` and `null`
are indistinguishable from absent and so are not worth a 400.

## result
`{ignored: string[], refused: [{key, why}]}` — `ignored` sorted, so the value echoed
back to callers and asserted in tests is stable.

# func report {#report}

A method of `ParamReporter`.

## arg model
The agent name, so the log line says which model the caller was aiming at.

## arg ignored
Names from `classify`.

## result
The same array, for echoing as `x_acp2api.ignored`.

## error unsupported_parameter
Thrown only when the mode is `error`. Status 400.

## invariant One log line per (model, parameter), for the process lifetime
Not per request. A client looping with `temperature` would otherwise produce one
line per call forever, and the interesting event is the first one.

# test params

- style parameters (`temperature`, `top_p`, `seed`) are ignored, never refused
- contract parameters (`tools`, `tool_choice`, `response_format`, `functions`, `audio`) are refused
- the `tools` refusal explains where tools actually come from (`mcpServers`)
- `n: 1` and `n: null` pass; `n: 3` is refused
- an unknown future field is ignored rather than refused
- native and emulated names appear in neither list
- the reporter logs each (model, parameter) pair exactly once across repeated calls
- mode `ignore` logs nothing; mode `error` throws with status 400 and the right code
